          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My son has been wheezing at night for a month and his blue inhaler is running out fast. Should he be seen urgently, and what does asthma guidance describe about stepping up treatment?",
          "answer": "Given that your son is wheezing nightly and using his blue inhaler frequently, it would be reasonable to discuss this with a clinician (diagnostic-specialist). If symptoms are severe, worsening, or new and unexplained, you should seek medical advice (diagnostic-specialist). Prompt medical assessment is advised if an asthma attack does not respond to reliever treatment or returns within a few hours (condition-asthma).\n\nThis pattern suggests his asthma may not be well-controlled with his current treatment (diagnostic-specialist). Asthma guidance describes a stepwise approach to treatment that is reviewed regularly based on symptom control and history of exacerbations (guideline-asthma-diagnosis-and-stepwise-therapy, research-specialist).\n\nBefore stepping up treatment, it is important to check inhaler technique and adherence, as these can significantly impact treatment effectiveness. Addressing these factors might resolve apparent treatment failure without needing to step up medication (diagnostic-specialist, research-specialist).\n\nLower steps of treatment might involve an as-needed inhaled corticosteroid with a fast-onset long-acting bronchodilator, or regular low-dose inhaled corticosteroid with an as-needed reliever (diagnostic-specialist, research-specialist). Middle steps could involve regular low-dose inhaled corticosteroid with a long-acting bronchodilator. In some regimens, the same combination inhaler is used as the reliever (diagnostic-specialist, research-specialist). Higher steps may include medium- or high-dose combination therapy, or the addition of a long-acting muscarinic antagonist, potentially leading to a referral for severe-asthma assessment, including consideration of biologic agents (guideline-asthma-diagnosis-and-stepwise-therapy, research-specialist). Stepping down treatment can be considered after three or more months of good control (research-specialist).\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
